\begin_layout Chapter
Static and modal analysis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Static analysis of free reeds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Static shape
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Modal analysis of free reeds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A free reed is a steel tongue riveted over a slot, a few tens of millimetres long and a few tenths of a millimetre thick, often scraped thin near the rivet and loaded with a heavier tip. Its first bending mode sets the pitch; the next ones and the torsion decide how the tongue answers the jet. This section describes how these modes were computed with the finite element method, how the computation was checked against exact solutions, and why one earlier result, kept for years as a reference, turned out to be wrong. The finite element model is passive: it gives the modes of the tongue in vacuo and the acoustic response of the chamber seen from the slot. The jet through the slot, non-linear and one-way, stays in the semi-analytical model, which receives these results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All computations use Elmer 26.2 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "elmer_models"
literal "true"

\end_inset
, meshed with Gmsh 4.15 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "geuzaine2009gmsh"
literal "true"

\end_inset
; the scripts are in research/fem of the repository, and every number below is regenerated by them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Geometry, material and boundary conditions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A tongue is described in the reed bank as a series of sections from the rivet to the tip, each with its length, its width and its thickness at both ends, and its material; a tip mass can be added. The reference tongue of this chapter is the one Ewen measured in 2020 for the reedgui tool (matrix.txt): four sections over a free length of 37.4 mm, widths from 4.26 to 3.47 mm, thicknesses of 0.35, 0.20 and 0.275 mm, and a 1.5 mm thick tip block over the last 9 mm. Spring steel is taken with 
\begin_inset Formula $E=210$
\end_inset
 GPa, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=7800$
\end_inset
 kg/m
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu=0.29$
\end_inset
; handbook values vary by about 3 per cent on 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset
 with the grade.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two clampings are compared. The ideal one fixes the whole cross-section at the root of the slot. The realistic one, called rivet clamping, extends the heel 5 mm behind the slot and holds only its lower face over a 2 mm band centred on the rivet, as the plate does. The realistic clamping lowers 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{1}$
\end_inset
 by 0.3 per cent: small, but larger than the remaining discretisation error, so it is the default. The eigenproblem is linear elasticity, solved with the direct solver Umfpack (MUMPS is not shipped with Elmer 26.2).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Elements and mesh convergence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The tongue is meshed with a structured grid of 20-node quadratic hexahedra: 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{x}$
\end_inset
 elements along the tongue (size 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{x}$
\end_inset
), 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{y}$
\end_inset
 across the width and 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{z}$
\end_inset
 through the thickness. Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:convergence"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
 gives the first three bending frequencies for three meshes.
\end_layout

