\begin_layout Subsection
(a) The tongue alone
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The plate is held in a vice by an edge, the slot free on both sides: the configuration computed by Elmer, apart from the air around the blade. That air adds a mass; for a strip of width 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset
 the added mass per unit length is of order 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{a}\pi b^{2}/4$
\end_inset
, against 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{s}bh$
\end_inset
 for the steel, a ratio of about 
\begin_inset Formula $2\times10^{-3}$
\end_inset
 for a 4 mm wide, 0.25 mm thick tongue, hence a frequency lower by 0.1 to 0.3 per cent (an estimate). Five plucks at the tip, then five at a corner of the tip to excite the torsion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What is calibrated. The first frequency calibrates a common thickness factor, with the modulus of steel kept: a hundredth of a millimetre on 0.25 mm moves 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{1}$
\end_inset
 by 4 per cent, while the modulus of a spring steel varies by 2 to 3 per cent. The equivalent apparent modulus is also given; outside 190 to 215 GPa it means that a dimension must be measured again. The damping ratios of the first two bending modes replace the guessed value of the time-domain model, which now accepts one value per mode. They are the damping without blowing (material, air, clamping), not the damping in play.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What is judged. A wrong thickness factor or a wrong modulus multiplies all frequencies by the same number, so the ratios 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{2}/f_{1}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{3}/f_{1}$
\end_inset
 do not depend on them. If they differ from the model by more than 3 per cent, the shape is wrong (tip mass, scraped profile, free length, clamping), not the thickness. The comparison pairs the bending modes first: the in-plane bending of the bass tongue (996 Hz in Elmer) lies close to the second bending mode (842 Hz) but is hardly excited by a pluck normal to the blade.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
(b) The tongue on its chamber
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The plate is laid on its chamber with the tested tongue outside, since the inner one cannot be reached once mounted, and the valve of that tongue removed or lifted: lying on the slot on the chamber side, it would decouple the chamber. The table hole is open. The tongue, of modal mass 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset
, stiffness 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 and swept area 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset
, pushes a flow 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma\dot{y}$
\end_inset
 into the Elmer network, which returns a pressure. Without losses, with 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{f}$
\end_inset
 the inertance of the slot, 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{t}$
\end_inset
 that of the hole, 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{H}$
\end_inset
 the chamber resonance and 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset
 the share of the swept volume that really goes through the chamber rather than around the tongue through the slot clearance,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
k-\omega^{2}m-\omega^{2}(\kappa\gamma)^{2}\left[L_{f}+\frac{L_{t}}{1-\omega^{2}/\omega_{H}^{2}}\right]=0.
\]

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In words: below the chamber resonance, the air the tongue pushes into the chamber and through the hole adds mass, and the note goes slightly down. Multiplying by 
\begin_inset Formula $1-\omega^{2}/\omega_{H}^{2}$
\end_inset
 gives a quadratic in 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega^{2}$
\end_inset
 with two roots, the tongue and the air resonator. With 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa=1$
\end_inset
 and the Elmer networks, the tongue is predicted 1.2 cents lower on its chamber for the bass reed, 2.8 cents for the medium and 3.5 cents for the high one, and the resonators at 1140, 1453 and 1574 Hz. These shifts are small: the plucked frequency is measured to better than 0.3 cent, but remounting the plate can move it more, so the tongue is recorded alone and on its chamber three times, alternately.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What is calibrated. 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa^{2}$
\end_inset
, as the ratio of the measured to the predicted shift (the shift is nearly linear in 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa^{2}$
\end_inset
); the effective length of the table hole, from the measured frequency of the air resonator, with the volume kept from the CAD (the end correction of the hole is the least certain dimension); and the loss resistance of the resonator, absent from the lossless finite element model, from its damping: for a series R-L-C, 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta_{H}=(R/2)\sqrt{C/L_{t}}$
\end_inset
. What would prove the model wrong: a shift of the opposite sign, or more than three times the prediction, or a resonator more than 10 per cent away from Elmer. The coupling through the slot would then have to be reformulated, not re-tuned.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
(c) The wind tunnel
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The plate is mounted as for playing, valve in place, on the wind chest. The pressure sensor, an XGZP6847 reading the absolute pressure, is connected to a static tap in a wall of the chest, away from the jet. The overpressure is the reading minus a zero taken with the blower stopped, before and after the series. Two cheap checks come first: ten readings at rest give the noise of the sensor, which bounds the step of the staircase; a water U-tube in parallel on the chest (1 mm of water is 9.81 Pa) checks the scale once. An absolute sensor with a full scale of about 100 kPa may have a noise of a few pascals, while the threshold of a bass reed is predicted between 10 and 50 Pa depending on the model; for the bass reeds the U-tube may be the better instrument.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The sensor and the microphone do not share a clock, so the thresholds are measured by a staircase noted by hand rather than by synchronised ramps. The pressure is raised by steps of about 5 per cent of the threshold (and more than the sensor noise), held three seconds each, and each step is noted as sounding or not; then the pressure is lowered the same way. Three cycles are made. The threshold lies between two steps, with half a step as uncertainty. Going up and then down is essential: going up alone cannot tell a subcritical from a supercritical bifurcation. Five-second sounds are then recorded at 1.2, 1.5 and 2 times the threshold (fem/souffle.py reads the sheet and the sounds).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What is calibrated: the rest lift of the tip, the least certain dimension at the calliper, adjusted so that the model gives the measured onset pressure 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\mathrm{on}}$
\end_inset
. What is judged, not calibrated: the ratio 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\mathrm{on}}/p_{\mathrm{off}}$
\end_inset
 (the hysteresis), the playing frequency and the amplitude law. The sharpest test is the playing frequency compared with the plucked frequency of the same tongue on its chamber: the closed-cavity models play above it, the network with the hole below it. No hysteresis at all within the steps, or a playing frequency above the plucked one, would contradict the present formulation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
What this protocol does not do yet
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The network is lossless and has one resonator; the resistance calibrated in (b) is not yet in coupled_reeds, whose orifice only has a quadratic loss.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The valve is a factor in its own right; it is in no model, and gesture (b) removes it to isolate the chamber.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The amplitude law needs the tip excursion, not the microphone level: it calls for the stroboscopic photograph of experiment E6.
\end_layout

